{"id": 141, "stem": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-text\"><span><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-100\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">A</mark> 23-year-old man comes to the office due to chest discomfort that usually occurs during exercise, such as jogging or climbing stairs.\u00a0 The symptoms go away 5 to 10 minutes after he stops.\u00a0 The patient has not had syncope but mentions some shortness of breath that accompanies the chest pain.\u00a0 Family history includes an uncle who died suddenly at age 35.\u00a0 Blood pressure is 122/70 mm Hg and pulse is 70/min and regular.\u00a0 The apical impulse is strong and sustained.\u00a0 While supine, the patient has a soft crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the apex and left sternal border; it becomes quite pronounced when he stands.\u00a0 Which of the following medications should be avoided while treating this patient's condition?\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c</p></span></div>", "options": [{"label": "A", "text": "Amiodarone"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Disopyramide"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Isosorbide dinitrate"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Metoprolol"}, {"label": "E", "text": "Verapamil"}], "correctAnswer": "C", "explanation": "<div class=\"relative w-full min-w-0 max-w-full self-stretch\" id=\"question-explanation\"><span class=\"\"><div id=\"div_t44603\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><div class=\"group/triggerable\" data-is-open=\"false\"><div class=\"group/draggable\" data-is-draggable=\"true\" data-is-dragging=\"false\"><div class=\"group/resizable relative\" data-is-maximized=\"false\" data-is-resizable=\"true\" data-is-resizing=\"false\"><div class=\"relative max-w-full\"><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute left-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-r from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"pointer-events-none absolute right-0 top-0 bottom-0 w-8 z-10 transition-opacity duration-200 bg-gradient-to-l from-white dark:from-nbme-primary-dark-800 warm:from-egg-sour-50 to-transparent opacity-0\"></div><div class=\"max-w-full overflow-x-auto [&amp;_td]:min-w-[8rem] [&amp;_th]:min-w-[8rem]\"><table class=\"border bg-transparent dark:text-white border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white hover:bg-transparent warm:hover:bg-transparent dark:hover:bg-transparent cursor-default mt-2 mb-3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tbody style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" colspan=\"4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><mark class=\"bg-transparent dark:text-neutral-50\" data-markjs=\"true\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">E</mark>ffect of maneuvers on hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 22%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Physiologic effect</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 22%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Left ventricular blood volume</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"width: 22%; font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Murmur intensity</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Valsalva (strain phase)</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" rowspan=\"3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">\u2193 Preload</p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" rowspan=\"3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">\u2193</p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" rowspan=\"3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">\u2191</p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Abrupt standing</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Nitroglycerin administration</strong></p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Sustained hand grip</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">\u2191 Afterload</p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" rowspan=\"3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">\u2191</p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" rowspan=\"3\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">\u2193</p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Squatting</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">\u2191 Afterload &amp; preload</p></td></tr><tr class=\"hover:bg-original-nbme-400/10 warm:hover:bg-brown-700/10 dark:hover:bg-white/15\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Passive leg raise</strong></p></td><td class=\"border border-collapse border-black warm:border-brown-800 dark:border-white p-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">\u2191 Preload</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></div></div></div></div><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">This patient with exertional chest pain, family history of sudden cardiac death, and a crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur that intensifies on standing most likely has <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">hypertrophic cardiomyopathy</strong> (HCM), an autosomal dominant inherited condition involving mutations in cardiac sarcomere proteins.\u00a0 The clinical manifestations of HCM result mostly from enlargement of the intraventricular septum that leads to <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">left ventricular outflow tract (LVOT) obstruction</strong>.\u00a0 Patients may be asymptomatic or can experience exertional dyspnea, fatigue, anginal pain, lightheadedness, or syncope; sudden cardiac arrest (or death) can occur due to reduced cardiac output and ventricular arrhythmia.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">The <a class=\"hint\" data-exhibit-id=\"exhibit-wapq25zz0srzr13w86x0rt2l\" href=\"#exhibit-exhibit-wapq25zz0srzr13w86x0rt2l\">degree of LVOT obstruction</a> in HCM varies with changes in left ventricular (LV) blood volume.\u00a0 Low LV blood volume collapses the LVOT and worsens the obstruction; therefore, <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">medications that decrease LV blood volume </strong>should be <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">avoided</strong>.\u00a0 These medications include the following:</p><ul class=\"flex flex-col justify-start items-start list-inside list-disc ml-4 mb-4\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start inline\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Preload reducers:</strong>\u00a0 Venous dilators (eg, <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">nitrates</strong>) and diuretics (eg, furosemide) reduce venous return to the heart and decrease LV end-diastolic volume.</p></li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start inline\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Afterload reducers:</strong>\u00a0 Arterial dilators (eg, hydralazine) facilitate increased stroke volume, which decreases LV end-systolic volume.</p></li><li class=\"mb-1\" style=\"font-size: 1rem;\"><p class=\"mb-4 text-start inline\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Balanced vasodilators:</strong>\u00a0 Combined venous and arterial dilators (eg, dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, ACE inhibitors) can worsen LVOT obstruction via reductions in preload and afterload.</p></li></ul><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\">In contrast, negative chronotropic agents (which reduce heart rate, increasing diastolic filling time) and negative inotropic agents (which reduce contractility, decreasing the systolic ejection fraction) help improve LVOT obstruction by increasing LV volumes.\u00a0 Beta blockers (eg, metoprolol) and nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (eg, verapamil) have negative chronotropic and inotropic effects and are used for treating HCM <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choices D and E)</strong>.\u00a0 Disopyramide, a class I antiarrhythmic (ie, sodium channel blocker) that has negative inotropic effects, is also effective via the same mechanism <strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice B)</strong>.</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">(Choice A)</strong>\u00a0 Amiodarone is a class III antiarrhythmic (potassium channel blocker) that may be used in patients with HCM who develop arrhythmia complications (eg, atrial fibrillation, ventricular tachycardia).</p><p class=\"mb-4 text-start\" style=\"margin-bottom: 1rem; font-weight: 400; font-size: 1rem;\"><strong style=\"font-size: 1rem;\">Educational objective:</strong>Left ventricular outflow tract obstruction in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy worsens with decreased left ventricular blood volume.\u00a0 Therefore, medications that decrease preload (eg, nitrates), afterload (eg, hydralazine), or both (eg, dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, ACE inhibitors) should be avoided.\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c</p></span></div>", "educationalObjective": "Left ventricular outflow tract obstruction in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy worsens with decreased left ventricular blood volume.  Therefore, medications that decrease preload (eg, nitrates), afterload (eg, hydralazine), or both (eg, dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, ACE inhibitors) should be avoided.\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u200c\u200b\u200c\u200b\u200b\u200b\u200c\u200c\u